<title>DUBAI: Saudi signs $35 mln U.S., India rice deal - paper.</title>
<headline>Saudi signs $35 mln U.S., India rice deal - paper.</headline>
<dateline>DUBAI 1997-03-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Saudi Arabia has signed a 130 million riyal ($34.6 million) deal to purchase about 60,000 tonnes of rice from the United States and India to meet increasing demand, a Saudi newspaper said on Saturday.</p>
<p>The English-language Arab News daily quoted Muhammad ibn Issa al-Jaber, chief executive of rice importing company Ajwaa RMTI, as saying the agreement had been reached on Friday.</p>
<p>&quot;About 60,000 tonnes of American and Indian rice valued at 130 million riyals will arrive in the kingdom within eight weeks at Jeddah Islamic Port to meet increasing Saudi demands,&quot; the paper quoted al-Jaber as saying.</p>
<p>Saudi Arabia imports around 700,000 tonnes of rice a year mainly from India, the United States, Thailand and Pakistan at a cost of about three billion riyals ($800 million).</p>
<p>The Arab News said demand for rice in the kingdom was increasing by 15 to 25 percent every year. ($1=3.75 riyals)</p>
</text>
